WEBVTT

00:00:00.193 --> 00:00:05.118
Optical flow asks how far the content at
each pixel moved between two frames.

00:00:05.118 --> 00:00:06.505
The basic assumption:

00:00:06.505 --> 00:00:09.130
a point keeps its brightness as it moves.

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:13.793
Take a pixel on a ramp, where brightness
rises by one per pixel to the right.

00:00:13.793 --> 00:00:16.217
If the content slides two pixels right,

00:00:16.218 --> 00:00:20.381
this pixel now shows what was two to its
left, which is two darker.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.518
Expand to first order, and you get one
linear equation per pixel.

00:00:24.455 --> 00:00:26.668
One equation, two unknowns.

00:00:26.668 --> 00:00:31.130
In the plane of u and v, one pixel only
says the answer lies on this line.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:32.655
We can pin down one piece:

00:00:32.655 --> 00:00:35.118
the normal flow, along the gradient.

00:00:35.118 --> 00:00:38.968
But two, one and minus two, four fit
equally well.

00:00:38.968 --> 00:00:43.618
Through a small hole, a bar looks the same
whether or not it slides along itself.

00:00:43.618 --> 00:00:45.530
This is the aperture problem.

00:00:45.512 --> 00:00:49.949
Lucas and Kanade assume that the flow is
the same across a small window.

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:52.374
Now every pixel adds its own line.

00:00:52.374 --> 00:00:54.712
Two pixels see a gradient to the right.

00:00:54.712 --> 00:00:56.424
Two see one downwards.

00:00:56.424 --> 00:00:58.412
The lines cross at one point:

00:00:58.412 --> 00:00:59.824
two, one.

00:00:59.824 --> 00:01:02.726
The least squares solve is a two by two
system,

00:01:02.727 --> 00:01:05.938
and its matrix is the structure tensor
from corners.

00:01:05.922 --> 00:01:09.747
Now suppose all four pixels have the same
gradient, pointing right.

00:01:09.747 --> 00:01:11.234
What happens to the solve?

00:01:11.234 --> 00:01:12.897
Pause and think about M.

00:01:14.498 --> 00:01:18.760
M becomes four, zero, zero, zero, which is
singular.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:22.148
All four lines are the same line, so
nothing fixes v.

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:25.810
The aperture problem is a rank-deficient
structure tensor.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:28.576
That's why a KLT tracker follows corners,

00:01:28.577 --> 00:01:33.299
where the smaller eigenvalue is large and
the flow is well determined.

00:01:33.223 --> 00:01:36.486
The linear step only holds for motions of
about a pixel.

00:01:36.486 --> 00:01:40.032
Each pass is really a Gauss-Newton step,
so we can iterate,

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:42.137
but an eight pixel jump is too far.

00:01:42.136 --> 00:01:43.861
A pyramid fixes that.

00:01:43.861 --> 00:01:48.497
Halving the image halves every
displacement, so eight pixels are one
pixel,

00:01:48.498 --> 00:01:49.487
three levels up.

00:01:49.486 --> 00:01:54.336
Estimate there, double it, warp the next
level, and solve for the small correction.

00:01:54.336 --> 00:01:56.548
Repeat down to full resolution.
